# GNOME Shell extension conventions

`best-practices.md` in the repository root is the upstream GNOME/EGO guidance for
AI-generated extensions and overrides general JavaScript habits. Read it before
changing extension code.

## Target exactly GNOME Shell 50

No compatibility shims, no `typeof x === 'function'` guards, no optional chaining
on values that cannot be null. Use `TextDecoder`, `console.error` and ESM
directly.

## Process isolation

- `extension.js` and `shell/` run in GNOME Shell.
- `lib/` is shell-independent and must **never** import `St`, `Clutter`, `Gtk`,
  `Gdk` or `Adw`, so the test suite can run it under plain `gjs`.

Keep `extension.js` limited to `enable()`/`disable()`, kept adjacent, with new
logic going into a module.

## Resource ownership

A class cleans up whatever it created, in `destroy()`, in this order: GLib
sources, signal handlers, child references, then `super.destroy()` last. Never
guard with a `this._destroyed` flag; drop the reference after destroying.

```js
destroy() {
    if (this._tickId) {
        GLib.Source.remove(this._tickId);
        this._tickId = 0;
    }
    Main.layoutManager.disconnect(this._monitorsChangedId);
    super.destroy();
}
```

`LevelSocket` owns its `Gio.Cancellable` and reconnect timeout; cancelling ends
the read loop, which then closes the connection itself (closing a stream with a
pending read throws).

## voxtype socket protocol

Frames are 16 bytes in native byte order: `u32 seq, f32 min, f32 max,
f32 peak_dbfs`, at 100 Hz, only while recording. The source of truth is
`src/audio/levels.rs` in voxtype. Reads can split a frame; `FrameDecoder`
keeps the remainder.

## Comments

Only document constraints the code cannot express, such as why recording state
is inferred from frame gaps or why `trackFullscreen` is false. No line-by-line
narration.
